  "content": {
    "id": "p7-q16-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Two years ago, a man’s age was three times the square of his son’s age. In three years, his age will be four times his son’s age. Find the present ages of the man and his son.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Man: $29$ years; son: $5$ years.\n\nModel their ages at each stated time and reject the root giving a negative age two years ago.",
      "worked": "Let the son's present age be $s$ years and the man's present age be $m$ years.\n$$\\begin{align*}m+3&=4(s+3)\\\\m&=4s+9\\\\m-2&=3(s-2)^2\\\\4s+7&=3(s-2)^2\\\\4s+7&=3s^2-12s+12\\\\3s^2-16s+5&=0\\\\(3s-1)(s-5)&=0\\\\s&=\\frac13\\text{ or }5\\end{align*}$$\nReject $s=\\frac13$, since the son's age two years ago would be negative. Thus $s=5$ and $m=4(5)+9=29$.\nTwo years ago their ages were $27$ and $3$, with $27=3(3^2)$. In three years their ages will be $32$ and $8$, with $32=4(8)$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 110
  },
